---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import MortgageRates from '@/components/tools/reference/MortgageRates.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="current-mortgage-rates">
  <MortgageRates client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Where current mortgage rates come from</h2>
    <p>
      The current mortgage rates on this page are the weekly national averages of the Freddie Mac
      Primary Mortgage Market Survey. Freddie Mac collects the rates that lenders offered for
      conventional, conforming, fully amortising home purchase loans to borrowers who put 20% down
      and have excellent credit. The survey started in April 1971 for 30-year loans and in August
      1991 for 15-year loans.
    </p>
    <h2>30-year and 15-year fixed rates</h2>
    <p>
      A 30-year loan has a lower monthly payment and more interest in total. A 15-year loan usually
      has a lower rate, a higher payment and much less total interest. The payment example under the
      chart uses the standard formula
      <code>payment = P × r / (1 − (1 + r)^−n)</code>, with the monthly rate <code>r</code> and the
      number of payments <code>n</code>.
    </p>
    <h2>Why your offer can be different</h2>
    <p>
      Lenders price each loan from your credit score, down payment, debt-to-income ratio, loan size and
      the points you pay. Jumbo, FHA, VA and adjustable-rate loans have their own rates. Compare the
      annual percentage rate (APR) of offers on the same day, because rates change daily.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
